# Lesson 3, new exercise: count the winners at no-trump. Nine sure tricks: take them.
title: Nine sure tricks
id: L03-03
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2
student: S
board: 35
dealer: S
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K72    965    AKQ4   764
  E  QJT9   32     T987   QJ9
  S  A63    A74    J3     AK852
  W  854    KQJT8  652    T3

auction:
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 16 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT with 16 points, and partner raised to game. You are
  declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the
  first trick.

planning:
  winners: 9-11
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 1 = The ace. Once it's gone, West's hearts are ready to run.
  diamonds: 4 = Four on top. Play the jack first, from the short side.
  clubs: 2-4 = Two on top. Two more if you give up a club, but that lets the opponents in.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: If you lose the lead, what will the opponents do?
play:
  1   W HK  N H5  E H2  S HA
      note: Count first: two spades, one heart, four diamonds and two clubs. That's nine, exactly enough. Take them before the opponents can take theirs.
  2   S DJ  W D2  N D4  E D7
      note: Start with diamonds. Play the jack first, the high card from the short side, so the suit doesn't get stuck in your hand.
  3   S D3  W D5  N DA  E D8
      note: Now over to dummy's diamonds.
  4   N DK  E D9  S C2  W D6
      note: Keep taking the diamonds.
  5   N DQ  E DT  S H4  W S4
      note: The ♦Q makes four diamond tricks.
  6   N SK  E S9  S S3  W S5
      note: Now the spades, starting with dummy's king.
  7   N S2  E ST  S SA  W S8
      note: Back to your ♠A.
  8   S CA  W C3  N C4  E C9
      note: And your two top clubs.
  9   S CK  W CT  N C6  E CJ
      note: That's nine tricks.
  10  S C5  W H8  N C7  E CQ
      note: Nine tricks are in. The rest belong to the defense.
  11  E H3  S H7  W HQ  N H9
  12  W HJ  N H6  E SJ  S S6
  13  W HT  N S7  E SQ  S C8
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count your winners. With nine on top, exactly enough, you take them
  and don't give the opponents a chance.

  This doesn't happen often. If you had started by giving up a club trick to
  set up your long clubs, West would have won the lead and taken four hearts
  to go with it: down one.
